"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]\n\ni.e. for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of *sunny* lines in a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n2. **Key Lemma.**  \n   For every integer \\(m\\ge 2\\) any covering of \\(S_m\\) by exactly \\(m\\) distinct lines contains **at most one** sunny line.\n\n   *Proof idea.*  \n   \u2013 The points on the \u201chypotenuse\u2019\u2019  \n\n   \\[\n   H_m=\\{(a,b)\\in S_m\\mid a+b=m+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise joined by lines of slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(H_m\\).  \n   \u2013 If a covering contains a sunny line, the line \\(x+y=m+1\\) (which is not sunny) must be present, otherwise the \\(m\\) points of \\(H_m\\) would require at least \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines, leaving no line for the interior points.  \n   \u2013 Removing the line \\(x+y=m+1\\) and the points of \\(H_m\\) reduces the problem to covering \\(S_{m-1}\\) with \\(m-1\\) lines. By induction on \\(m\\) the reduced covering can have at most one sunny line, whence the original covering also has at most one sunny line.\n\n3. **Consequences.**  \n   The lemma implies that for any \\(n\\ge3\\) a covering by \\(n\\) lines can have at most one sunny line; thus the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).\n\n4. **Realisation of the two possibilities.**  \n\n   \u2013 *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \n\n   \u2013 *\\(k=1\\).*  We construct such a covering by induction.  \n     *Base case \\(n=3\\).*  The three lines  \n\n     \\[\n     L_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;x+y=4,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=2x\n     \\]\n\n     cover all points of \\(S_3\\); only \\(L_3\\) is sunny.  \n     *Inductive step.*  Assume a covering of \\(S_{n-1}\\) by \\(n-1\\) lines with exactly one sunny line exists.  Add the non\u2011sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     D_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1,\n     \\]\n\n     which covers all points of the hypotenuse \\(H_n\\).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines (the inductive covering) cover the interior \\(S_{n-1}\\) and contain exactly one sunny line.  Hence we obtain a covering of \\(S_n\\) with exactly one sunny line.\n\n   Thus both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nH_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma  \nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 2\\) any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_m\\) contains at most one sunny line.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n**Base case \\(m=2\\).**  \n\\(S_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line through two of these points is either horizontal (\\(y=1\\) or \\(y=2\\)), vertical (\\(x=1\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=3\\)); all are non\u2011sunny.  Consequently a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(S_2\\).  Since two lines are available, a covering of \\(S_2\\) can contain at most one sunny line.  The lemma holds for \\(m=2\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement true for \\(m-1\\) (\\(m\\ge3\\)).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a covering of \\(S_m\\) by \\(m\\) distinct lines.\n\nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains no sunny line, the lemma is satisfied.  \nSuppose \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line.  \n\n*Claim 1.*  The line  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+1}:\\;x+y=m+1\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof of Claim 1.*  \nAll points of the hypotenuse \\(H_m\\) have the form \\((a,m+1-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le m\\).  \nAny line through two distinct points of \\(H_m\\) has slope\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(m+1-a')-(m+1-a)}{a'-a}=\\frac{a-a'}{a'-a}=-1,\n\\]\n\nhence is non\u2011sunny.  Therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(H_m\\).  \nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) did not contain the line \\(D_{m+1}\\), each point of \\(H_m\\) would have to be covered by a distinct non\u2011sunny line (horizontal or vertical), because any such line meets \\(H_m\\) in at most one point.  This would require at least \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines, leaving no line for the interior points \\(S_m\\setminus H_m\\).  Hence (1) must be present in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \u220e\n\nRemove the line \\(D_{m+1}\\) from \\(\\mathcal L\\) and delete the points of \\(H_m\\).  \nThe remaining points are exactly\n\n\\[\nS_{m-1}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m\\},\n\\]\n\nand the remaining lines form a family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(m-1\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_{m-1}\\).  \nSince \\(D_{m+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny, the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) equals the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nBy the induction hypothesis, \\(\\mathcal L'\\) contains at most one sunny line; consequently \\(\\mathcal L\\) also contains at most one sunny line.\n\nThus the lemma holds for all \\(m\\ge2\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence for the original problem\n\nFor the given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of points to be covered is \\(S_n\\).  \nApplying the lemma with \\(m=n\\) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\text{any covering of }S_n\\text{ by }n\\text{ lines contains at most one sunny line.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=1.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of coverings for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is non\u2011sunny, the lines are distinct, and a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on \\(L_b\\).  Thus all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and no line is sunny.  Therefore \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that a covering with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;x+y=4,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=2x\n\\]\n\ncover the six points of \\(S_3\\):\n\\(L_1\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\);\n\\(L_2\\) covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\);\n\\(L_3\\) passes through \\((1,2)\\) (the only point not yet covered).  \nOnly \\(L_3\\) has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence exactly one sunny line occurs.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume for some \\(n\\ge4\\) that there exists a covering \\(\\mathcal C_{n-1}\\) of \\(S_{n-1}\\) by \\(n-1\\) distinct lines containing exactly one sunny line.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non\u2011sunny and covers all points of the hypotenuse \\(H_n\\).  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n:=\\mathcal C_{\\,n-1}\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) are covered: the interior points \\(S_{n-1}\\) are covered by \\(\\mathcal C_{n-1}\\), and the points of \\(H_n\\) are covered by \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \nSince \\(D_{n+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny, the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) equals that in \\(\\mathcal C_{n-1}\\), namely one.  \n\nThus a covering with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\,1\\}}.\n\\]"
