n-1, n. So at most 2 points. So not many.\n\nThus the maximum number of points on a slope 1 line is achieved for c near 0, giving about floor((n+1)/2) points.\n\nThus the maximum number of points a sunny line can contain is floor((n+1)/2). Let's try to prove that for any allowed primitive direction (p,q) with |p|,|q|\u22651 and q \u2260 -p, the number of integer points in S on a line with that direction is at most floor((n+1)/2). Actually for direction (p,q) with |p|+|q|\u22653 maybe the bound is even lower.\n\nWe can attempt to prove a general bound: For any line L not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or x+y=0, the number of points of S on L is at most floor((n+1)/2). This is plausible because the triangle S is \"thin\" in the direction of any line not parallel to the sides of the triangle. The sides of the triangle are x=1 (vertical), y=1 (horizontal), and x+y=n+1 (slope -1). So any line not parallel to these sides will intersect the triangle in a segment whose projection onto the direction orthogonal to the side is limited.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a combinatorial argument: For any line L not parallel to any side, the set of points of S on L cannot contain two points that share the same x-coordinate or same y-coordinate or same sum a+b. Because if two points (a,b) and (a',b') are on L, then the vector (a'-a, b'-b) is a multiple of (p,q). Since p,q are nonzero and q \u2260 -p, the differences in x and y are both nonzero and not opposite. So the points have distinct x, distinct y, and distinct sums a+b. So each sunny line can contain at most one point from each vertical line x = const, each horizontal line y = const, and each diagonal line a+b = const. Since there are n possible x-values (1,...,n) and n possible y-values (1,...,n) and n possible sums (2,...,n+1). But that doesn't directly give a bound.\n\nBut we can use the pigeonhole principle: Since each sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the n vertical lines, the maximum number of points it can