ing (a,1) with (1,b) where a and b have opposite parity? Then \u0394x = 1-a, \u0394y = b-1. If a even, b even, then both differences odd? Actually if a even, b even, then \u0394x odd, \u0394y odd, gcd may be odd >1? Could be 1 or odd. But we need gcd=2 to get 3 points. So we need both differences even. So a,b both odd. So only odd a,b produce gcd even. So we have used (5,1)-(1,3) and (3,1)-(1,5). The remaining odd a,b pairs are (1,1) (vertical/horizontal) and (1,? ) etc. So we cannot get more lines of that type.\n\nThus we need other families of lines.\n\nConsider lines connecting a point on base (a,1) with a point on hypotenuse (c, n+1-c). The difference is (c-a, n-c). We need both components even to get gcd\u22652. Since n=6, n-c = 6-c. So we need c-a even and 6-c even => c even. So choose c even. Then c-a even => a even. So choose a even, c even. Then gcd( c-a, 6-c ) may be 2.\n\nLet's try c=4 (even), then point on hypotenuse is (4,3). Choose a=2 (even). Then line through (2,1) and (4,3) has \u0394x = 2, \u0394y = 2 => gcd=2 => line contains 3 integer points: (2,1),(3,2),(4,3). Are these interior? (2,1) sum=3 interior, (3,2) sum=5 interior, (4,3) sum=7 >6, not interior (it's on hypotenuse? Actually (4,3) sum=7, which is n+1=7, so it's on the hypotenuse, not interior). So line includes only 2 interior points (2,1) and (3,2). But (3,2) is interior. So not enough.\n\nBut we can consider line through (4,1) and (2,5) (hypotenuse point (2,5) sum=7). \u0394x = -2, \u0394y = 4 => gcd=2 => line includes (4,1),(3,3),(2,5). (3,3) sum=6 interior, (4,1) interior, (2,5) on hypotenuse. So line covers 2 interior points.\n\nThus lines connecting base to hypotenuse may not give 3 interior points.\n\nConsider lines connecting vertical side (1,b) to hypotenuse (c, n+1-c). Difference = (c-1, n+1-c - b). For both components even, need c odd? Actually c-1 even => c odd. And n+1-c - b even => (7 - c - b) even => (c + b) odd? Since 7 odd, subtract even => odd? Let's compute: 7 - c - b even => c + b odd. So if c odd, then b even. So choose c odd, b even. For n=6, c odd values: 1,3,5. c=5 => point (5,2) on hypotenuse. Choose b=2 (even). Then line through (1,2) and (5,2) is horizontal (unsunny). Not allowed. Choose b=4 (even). (1,4) and (5,2) difference (4, -2) => gcd=2 => line includes (1,4),(3,3),(5,2). (3,3) interior, (1,4) interior, (5,2) on hypotenuse. So line covers 2 interior points.\n\nThus not enough.\n\nThus maybe we need to use lines of slope -2 (\u0394x = -1, \u0394y = 2) that can contain 3 points if we have a longer segment: Actually slope -2 lines have \u0394x = -1, \u0394y = 2, gcd=1, so only 2 integer points. So not enough.\n\nThus maybe we need to use lines of slope -1/3 or -3? For gcd=2, we need \u0394x and \u0394y both multiples of 2, but not both multiples of 4. So slopes like -2 (\u0394x = -1, \u0394y = 2) not both even. So we need slopes where \u0394x and \u0394y are both even. That means the line passes through points with same parity (both coordinates even or both odd). So lines connecting points of same parity can have many integer points.\n\nThus we can consider lines connecting (odd,odd) points. The interior points have parity patterns: (a,b) both odd or one odd one even. Let's list interior points for n=6 with parity:\n\n(1,1) odd-odd\n(2,1) even-odd\n(3,1) odd-odd\n(4,1) even-odd\n(5,1) odd-odd\n(1,2) odd-even\n(2,2) even-even\n(3,2) odd-even\n(4,2) even-even\n(1,3) odd-odd\n(2,3) even-odd\n(3,3) odd-odd\n(1,4) odd-even\n(2,4) even-even\n(1,5) odd-odd\n\nThus there are many odd-odd points: (1,1),(3,1),(5,1),(1,3),(3,3),(1,5). That's 6 points. They lie on lines of slope -1? Actually they are on the same line x+y = even? Let's see: (1,1) sum=2, (3,1) sum=4, (5,1) sum=6, (1,3) sum=4, (3,3) sum=6, (1,5) sum=6. So they are not collinear except maybe some.\n\nBut we can pair odd-odd points to form lines with slope -1? Actually line through (1,1) and (3,1) is horizontal (unsunny). So not good.\n\nBut we can consider lines connecting odd-odd points that are not on same horizontal/vertical/diag. For example, line through (1,1) and (3,3) slope = (3-1)/(3-1) = 1, sunny. Points on that line: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Indeed (2,2) is interior even-even. So line L3: (1,1)-(2,2)-(3,3) covers 3 interior points. Good.\n\nSimilarly, line through (1,3) and (5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-1) = -2/4 = -1/2, sunny. Points: (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). That's L1 we already have.\n\nLine through (1,5) and (3,3) slope = (3-5)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, unsunny. Not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,5) and (5,1) slope = (1-5)/(5-1) = -4/4 = -1, unsunny.\n\nLine through (3,1) and (1,3) slope = (3-1)/(1-3) = 2/(-2) = -1, unsunny.\n\nLine through (3,1) and (5,1) horizontal unsunny.\n\nLine through (3,1) and (3,3) vertical unsunny.\n\nThus we have L3: (1,1)-(2,2)-(3,3). L1: (5,1)-(3,2)-(1,3). L2: (3,1)-(2,3)-(1,5). That's three lines covering 9 points. The remaining points are (2,1),(4,1),(1,2),(2,2) (already covered), (4,2),(1,4),(2,4). Actually (2,2) covered by L3. So remaining: (2,1),(4,1),(1,2),(4,2),(1,4),(2,4). That's 6 points left, need 2 lines each covering 3 points.\n\nWe can try to find lines covering these.\n\nConsider line through (2,1) and (4,2): slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2, sunny. Points on this line: (2,1),(3,1.5) not integer, (4,2). So only 2 integer points. Not enough.\n\nConsider line through (2,1) and (1,4): slope = (4-1)/(1-2) = 3/(-1) = -3, sunny. Points: (2,1),(1,4). Also (0,7) outside. So only 2 points.\n\nConsider line through (4,1) and (1,4): slope = (4-1)/(1-4) = 3/(-3) = -1, unsunny.\n\nConsider line through (4,1) and (2,4): slope = (4-1)/(2-4) = 3/(-2) = -1.5, sunny. Points: (4,1),(2,4). Also (6, -2) outside. So only 2 points.\n\nConsider line through (1,2) and (4,2): horizontal unsunny.\n\nConsider line through (1,2) and (2,4): slope = (4-2)/(2-1) = 2, sunny. Points: (1,2),(2,4). Also (3,6) outside. So only 2 points.\n\nThus we cannot find lines covering 3 points among these 6 points. Perhaps we need to adjust earlier lines to allow a different partition.\n\nThus constructing a partition for n=6 is nontrivial. But we suspect it's possible because the problem likely expects that all k up to the bound are possible.\n\nWe need to find a general construction for any n and any allowed k.\n\nLet's step back and think about the problem more abstractly.\n\nWe have a triangular lattice of size n. The points can be arranged in rows. The condition a+b \u2264 n+1 defines a triangular shape.\n\nWe need to cover these points with n lines. The lines can be of any slope, but we count sunny lines.\n\nWe need to find all possible k.\n\nWe have derived necessary condition: For even n, k \u2264 n-1; for odd n, k \u2264 n.\n\nNow we need to show that for each n, all k satisfying 0 \u2264 k \u2264 n (if n odd) or 0 \u2264 k \u2264 n-1 (if n even) are achievable.\n\nThus the answer likely is:\n\n- If n is odd, all k = 0,1,2,...,n are possible.\n- If n is even, all k = 0,1,2,...,n-1 are possible.\n\nWe need to prove existence for each k.\n\nWe already have constructions for k=0 (all horizontal lines). For k=n (odd n) we need a construction with all sunny lines. For k=n-1 (even n) we have a construction with one unsunny line (hypotenuse) and n-1 sunny lines (we need to show existence for all even n). For intermediate k, we can perhaps start from the all-horizontal configuration and replace some horizontal lines with sunny lines while preserving coverage.\n\nIdea: Use a \"swap\" operation: Replace a horizontal line y = b (unsunny) with a sunny line that passes through all points of that horizontal line except maybe one, and also passes through some other points that were previously covered by other horizontal lines, thereby reducing the number of unsunny lines by 1 and increasing sunny lines by 1, while maintaining coverage.\n\nAlternatively, we can construct a family of configurations parameterized by the number of unsunny lines u, using a \"staircase\" pattern: Use u horizontal lines for the top u rows (or bottom u rows), and use sunny lines to cover the rest.\n\nSpecifically, for any u (0 \u2264 u \u2264 n), we can use the u horizontal lines y = 1,2,...,u (unsunny). Then the remaining points are those with b > u, i.e., rows b = u+1,...,n. The number of remaining points is sum_{b=u+1}^{n} (n+1-b) = sum_{c=1}^{n-u} c = (n-u)(n-u+1)/2. We need to cover these points with n-u lines, all sunny. So we need to partition the smaller triangle of size n-u into n-u sunny lines.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: For any m = n-u (where m \u2265 0), can we cover the triangular set of points with side length m (i.e., points (a,b) with a,b \u2265 1, a+b \u2264 m+1) using exactly m lines, all sunny? If we can do that for any m, then we can achieve any u (i.e., any k = n-u). However, we need to ensure that the sunny lines used for the smaller triangle are distinct from the horizontal lines used earlier (they will be, as they have different slopes). Also we need to ensure that the sunny lines are not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or slope -1.\n\nThus the core subproblem: For any integer m \u2265 1, can we cover the triangular lattice of size m with exactly m lines, all sunny? If yes, then we can achieve any k = n - u for any u.\n\nBut we have a restriction: For even n, we cannot have u=0 (i.e., all sunny) because we proved k \u2264 n-1. So for even n, m = n (i.e., u=0) is not possible. So the subproblem fails for m even? Let's examine.\n\nThe subproblem is: For any m, can we cover the triangle of size m with m sunny lines? For m odd, maybe yes; for m even, maybe not. Indeed, we saw for n=4 (even), we cannot cover the triangle of size 4 with 4 sunny lines (since k \u2264 n-1). So the subproblem fails for even m. So the condition is: The triangle of size m can be covered by m sunny lines iff m is odd. For m even, we need at least one unsunny line.\n\nThus the general solution: For any n, we can choose u unsunny lines to cover the top u rows (horizontal lines), and then cover the remaining triangle of size m = n-u with m sunny lines if m is odd, or with m-1 sunny lines plus one unsunny line if m is even. This yields a recursion.\n\nThus we can achieve any k such that the number of sunny lines is at most n, and the parity constraints may restrict some values.\n\nLet's try to formalize.\n\nLet\u2019s define a configuration C(m) for each integer m \u2265 1:\n\n- If m is odd, there exists a covering of the triangle of size m using exactly m sunny lines (no unsunny lines). We'll construct such a covering.\n\n- If m is even, there exists a covering of the triangle of size m using exactly m-1 sunny lines and 1 unsunny line (e.g., the hypotenuse). So the number of sunny lines is m-1.\n\nThus for any m, we can cover the triangle of size m with at most m sunny lines, and the exact number of sunny lines is m if m odd, m-1 if m even.\n\nNow for the original problem with n, we can choose u unsunny lines (horizontal) covering the first u rows, and then cover the remaining triangle of size m = n-u using the above configuration. The total number of sunny lines will be:\n\n- If m is odd: k = m (since the covering of the remaining triangle uses m sunny lines) = n - u.\n\n- If m is even: k = m - 1 = n - u - 1.\n\nThus the total number of sunny lines k can be either n - u (if n-u odd) or n - u - 1 (if n-u even). Since u can range from 0 to n (subject to constraints), we can achieve many k.\n\nBut we need to find all possible k.\n\nLet's denote n-u = m. Then k = m if m odd, k = m-1 if m even. So k = m - (1 if m even else 0). Since m = n - u, we can rewrite:\n\nIf n - u is odd: k = n - u.\nIf n - u is even: k = n - u - 1.\n\nThus k can be any integer such that there exists u with 0 \u2264 u \u2264 n and k = n - u - \u03b5, where \u03b5 = 0 if n - u odd, \u03b5 = 1 if n - u even.\n\nEquivalently, k can be any integer from 0 to n, except possibly some values.\n\nLet's examine possible k for given n.\n\nLet\u2019s consider n parity.\n\nCase 1: n odd (n = 2t+1). Then n is odd.\n\nWe can choose u from 0 to n. For each u, m = n - u.\n\n- If m odd (i.e., n - u odd), then k = m = n - u.\n- If m even, then k = m - 1 = n - u - 1.\n\nThus k can be either n - u or n - u - 1, depending on parity of n - u.\n\nSince n is odd, parity of n - u is opposite to parity of u (odd - u). So:\n\n- If u even, then n - u is odd => k = n - u.\n- If u odd, then n - u is even => k = n - u - 1.\n\nThus as u runs from 0 to n, we get k values:\n\n- For u even (including 0): k = n - u (which is odd - even = odd). So k takes values n, n-2, n-4, ..., down to maybe 1 or 0 depending on parity.\n\n- For u odd: k = n - u - 1 = (n - 1) - u (since n odd). Since u odd, (n-1) is even, subtract odd => odd? Actually n-1 is even, subtract odd => odd? Let's compute: n=2t+1, u=2s+1 => k = (2t+1) - (2s+1) - 1 = 2t - 2s - 1 = 2(t - s) - 1, which is odd. So k is odd as well. So all k are odd? Wait, both cases produce odd k? Let's check with small n.\n\nTake n=5 (odd). According to this, possible k are odd numbers from 0? Let's compute.\n\nn=5. u can be 0..5.\n\n- u=0 (even): k = n - u = 5.\n- u=1 (odd): k = n - u - 1 = 5 - 1 - 1 = 3.\n- u=2 (even): k = 5 - 2 = 3.\n- u=3 (odd): k = 5 - 3 - 1 = 1.\n- u=4 (even): k = 5 - 4 = 1.\n- u=5 (odd): k = 5 - 5 - 1 = -1 (invalid). So stop at u=4.\n\nThus possible k values: 5,3,1. That's only odd numbers, not all numbers up to 5. But we earlier found for n=3 (odd) that k=0,1,2,3 are possible (including even). So this pattern is not correct.\n\nThus our construction using horizontal lines for the top rows may not be sufficient to achieve all k. We need more flexible constructions.\n\nLet's step back.\n\nWe need to find all possible k. Let's try to compute small n manually to see pattern.\n\nn=3: Points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). We found configurations for k=0 (all horizontal), k=1 (one sunny line), k=2 (two sunny lines), k=3 (three sunny lines?) Did we find k=3? We attempted but thought not possible. Let's verify if k=3 is possible for n=3.\n\