ed \u2013 a contradiction.  \nTherefore any covering of \\(S_2\\) by two lines contains at most one sunny line. \u220e\n\n#### Inductive step.\n\nAssume the lemma holds for \\(m-1\\) (\\(m\\ge3\\)).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a covering of \\(S_m\\) by \\(m\\) distinct lines.\n\nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains no sunny line, the lemma is satisfied.  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line.\n\n##### Claim 1.  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+1}:\\;x+y=m+1\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof of Claim 1.*  \nEvery point of the hypotenuse \\(H_m\\) has the form \\((a,m+1-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le m\\).  \nIf a line passes through two distinct points \\((a,m+1-a)\\) and \\((a',m+1-a')\\) of \\(H_m\\), its slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(m+1-a')-(m+1-a)}{a'-a}\n=\\frac{a-a'}{a'-a}=-1,\n\\]\n\nso the line is non\u2011sunny.  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(H_m\\).\n\nIf the line \\(D_{m+1}\\) were absent from \\(\\mathcal L\\), each point of \\(H_m\\) would have to be covered by a distinct non\u2011sunny line (since a sunny line can meet \\(H_m\\) in at most one point).  \nThus at least \\(|H_m|=m\\) non\u2011sunny lines would be required, exhausting the whole set of \\(m\\) lines and leaving no line to cover the interior points \\(S_m\\setminus H_m\\).  \nThis contradiction shows that \\(D_{m+1}\\) must be present in \\(\\mathcal L\\). \u220e\n\nRemove the line \\(D_{m+1}\\) from \\(\\mathcal L\\) and delete the points of \\(H_m\\).  \nThe remaining points are exactly \\(S_{m-1}\\), and the remaining lines form a family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(m-1\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_{m-1}\\).  \nSince \\(D_{m+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny, the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) equals the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nBy the induction hypothesis, \\(\\mathcal L'\\) contains at most one sunny line; consequently \\(\\mathcal L\\) also contains at most one sunny line.\n\nThus the lemma holds for all \\(m\\ge2\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence for the original problem\n\nFor the given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(S_n\\) as the set of points to be covered.  \nApplying the lemma with \\(m=n\\) yields\n\n\\[\n\\text{any covering of }S_n\\text{ by }n\\text{ distinct lines contains at most one sunny line.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=1.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of coverings for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\).\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is non\u2011sunny, the lines are distinct, and for any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).  \nThus all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and no line is sunny.  \nTherefore \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\).\n\nWe construct such a covering by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;x+y=4,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=2x\n\\]\n\ncover the six points of \\(S_3\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),\\\\\nL_2 &: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1),\\\\\nL_3 &: (1,2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nOnly \\(L_3\\) has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence exactly one sunny line occurs.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge4\\) there exists a covering \\(\\mathcal C_{\\,n-1}\\) of \\(S_{n-1}\\) by \\(n-1\\) distinct lines containing exactly one sunny line.  \nAdd the non\u2011sunny line  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through all points of the new hypotenuse \\(H_n\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n:=\\mathcal C_{\\,n-1}\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) is covered: points of \\(S_{n-1}\\) are covered by \\(\\mathcal C_{\\,n-1}\\), and points of \\(H_n\\) are covered by \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \nSince \\(D_{n+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny, \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) contains exactly the same sunny line as \\(\\mathcal C_{\\,n-1}\\), namely one sunny line.  \n\nThus a covering of \\(S_n\\) with exactly one sunny line exists for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\,1\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
