            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\n\\qquad \nH_m=\\{(a,b)\\in S_m\\mid a+b=m+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (base case \\(m=2\\))\n\n\\(S_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe three unordered pairs of distinct points of \\(S_2\\) determine the following lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)\\text{ and }(1,2) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; x=1 \\quad(\\text{slope } \\infty),\\\\\n&(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,1) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y=1 \\quad(\\text{slope } 0),\\\\\n&(1,2)\\text{ and }(2,1) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; x+y=3 \\quad(\\text{slope } -1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.  \nTherefore a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(S_2\\).  \nIf two sunny lines were used to cover \\(S_2\\), at most two points would be covered, leaving one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  Consequently any covering of \\(S_2\\) by two distinct lines contains at most one sunny line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (the hypotenuse line must appear)\n\nLet \\(m\\ge3\\) and let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of exactly \\(m\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_m\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+1}:\\;x+y=m+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWe claim \\(D_{m+1}\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\in H_m\\).  Any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that contains two distinct points of \\(H_m\\) must have slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(m+1-a')-(m+1-a)}{a'-a}\n=\\frac{a-a'}{a'-a}=-1,\n\\]\n\nhence it is non\u2011sunny and, because it contains two points of \\(H_m\\), it must be exactly the line \\(x+y=m+1\\), i.e. \\(D_{m+1}\\).\n\nNow let us examine the other possibilities for a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) that is not \\(D_{m+1}\\).\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, \\(\\ell\\) has equation \\(y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).  Intersecting with the line \\(x+y=m+1\\) yields the unique point \\((m+1-c,c)\\); thus \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(H_m\\) in at most one point.\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, \\(\\ell\\) has equation \\(x=c\\).  Intersecting with \\(x+y=m+1\\) gives the unique point \\((c,m+1-c)\\); again at most one point of \\(H_m\\).\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\) but is not \\(D_{m+1}\\), its equation is \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(d\\neq m+1\\).  Then \\(\\ell\\cap H_m=\\varnothing\\).\n\nThus every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) other than \\(D_{m+1}\\) can contain **at most one** point of \\(H_m\\).  Since \\(|H_m|=m\\), covering all points of \\(H_m\\) with only \\(m\\) lines forces one of the lines to contain two points of \\(H_m\\); the only line capable of this is \\(D_{m+1}\\).  Hence \\(D_{m+1}\\in\\mathcal L\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (induction: at most one sunny line)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge2\\) any covering of \\(S_m\\) by exactly \\(m\\) distinct lines contains at most one sunny line.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*  \n\n*Base case.*  \\(m=2\\) is Lemma\u202f1.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the statement holds for \\(m-1\\) (\\(m\\ge3\\)).  Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a covering of \\(S_m\\) by \\(m\\) distinct lines.  By Lemma\u202f2, \\(D_{m+1}\\in\\mathcal L\\).  Remove this line and delete the points of \\(H_m\\) from the covered set.  Because  \n\n\\[\nS_m\\setminus H_m=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le m\\}=S_{m-1},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nthe remaining \\(m-1\\) lines, call the collection \\(\\mathcal L'\\), cover all points of \\(S_{m-1}\\).  The lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) are still distinct (we removed only one line).  Moreover, no point of \\(S_{m-1}\\) lies on \\(D_{m+1}\\) (every point of \\(D_{m+1}\\) satisfies \\(a+b=m+1\\)), so every point of \\(S_{m-1}\\) remains covered by \\(\\mathcal L'\\).  By the induction hypothesis, \\(\\mathcal L'\\) contains at most one sunny line.  Since \\(D_{m+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny (its slope is \\(-1\\)), the total number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is the same as in \\(\\mathcal L'\\), i.e. at most one. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence for the original problem\n\nTake \\(m=n\\) in Lemma\u202f3.  Any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) can contain **at most one** sunny line.  Hence the only possible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=1.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of configurations for the two values of \\(k\\)\n\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(0\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.  The lines are distinct.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_b\\).  Thus all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines occur.\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nWe construct such a covering for every \\(n\\ge3\\) by induction.\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;x+y=4,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=2x .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\n\\(L_1\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\); \\(L_2\\) covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\); \\(L_3\\) covers \\((1,2)\\).  Hence all points of \\(S_3\\) are covered.  The slopes of \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) (non\u2011sunny), while the slope of \\(L_3\\) is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); thus exactly one sunny line appears.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume for some \\(n\\ge4\\) that there exists a covering \\(\\mathcal C_{\\,n-1}\\) of \\(S_{n-1}\\) by \\(n-1\\) distinct lines containing exactly one sunny line.  Define the line  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n\\(D_{n+1}\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.  Every point of \\(S_{n-1}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\); consequently none of the lines in \\(\\mathcal C_{\\,n-1}\\) can be equal to \\(D_{n+1}\\) (the latter contains only points with \\(a+b=n+1\\)).  Hence the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n:=\\mathcal C_{\\,n-1}\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  The new line \\(D_{n+1}\\) covers precisely the points of the hypotenuse  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhile the points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) form the set \\(S_{n-1}\\) and are covered by \\(\\mathcal C_{\\,n-1}\\).  Therefore \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) covers all of \\(S_n\\).  Since \\(D_{n+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny, the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) equals the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal C_{\\,n-1}\\), namely one.  This completes the induction.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a covering with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]\n\nBoth values are attainable, and no covering can contain more than one sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
